# the PE image writer: an executable from the image record of a link
#
# every address, file offset and size of an output section comes from the
# layout record, and every byte from the relocated record. the writer builds
# the PE/COFF model the PE/COFF writer turns into bytes. the headers are the
# MS-DOS header and program every PE linker writes, the PE signature, the file
# header, the optional header with its sixteen data directories and a section
# header for each section of the image, which the header measure sizes from
# the laid out image. the optional header layout is the one whose address is
# as wide as the architecture's, the machine is the architecture's COFF code,
# the image base the link's, the section and file alignment the layout rules',
# the subsystem the target's or else the host's, and the system version the
# target's, else the host's, else SYSTEM. the characteristics follow the
# layout and the output kind: an image the loader may place anywhere is
# dynamic base, and high entropy where its addresses are 64 bits wide. every
# executable is NX compatible and terminal server aware. the checksum is PE
# Format's, of the bytes written.
#
# a section no segment loads, as the debugging information a program database
# would hold, is no section of the image and is left out, as are output
# sections that hold no byte. .reloc is a table the image reserves before
# layout, measured from the link's loader relocation sites where layout puts
# them, and laid out last in a segment of its own: one block per 4 KiB page,
# in address order, each padded to a multiple of four bytes. the writer fills
# it from the same sites at the addresses relocate filled in, which take the
# bytes layout left. an image the loader may place anywhere and that holds no
# address to relocate still states dynamic base and not relocations
# stripped, with no .reloc and a zero base relocation directory, as there is
# nothing to fix up wherever the loader places it (PE Format, The .reloc
# Section).
#
# the inputs' function tables, .pdata, lie in one output section laid out and
# relocated as any other, each entry living with the function it names. the
# writer reads the relocated entries in the layout the machine's function
# table row states, sorts them by the address their function starts at, as
# the loader's binary search needs, and names them in the exception
# directory. an ARM64 entry's unwind word is kept as written, packed or the
# address of its .xdata. an image of two output sections of function tables,
# or of a machine with no function table layout, is refused.
#
# an image that imports holds, per DLL by rank, one import descriptor, its run
# of the import lookup table and of the import address table, each closed by
# an empty slot, a hint/name entry per import by name and the DLL's name. the
# import address table is the slot table the link reserves, and the
# descriptors, the lookup table, the hint/name table and the DLL names are a
# table the image reserves ahead of it in .idata, measured from the imports. a
# slot of either table holds the address of its import's hint/name entry, or
# the ordinal flag and the ordinal for an import by ordinal, as its short
# import object's name type says. an import whose input states no hint takes
# hint zero, which the loader reads as none. the import and import address
# table directories name the descriptors and the address table. a call to an
# import goes through the import's thunk, which the link writes from its
# role's template.
#
# the image's thread-local template is .tls, which collects the .tls$ input
# sections in name order, zero-fill ones behind the initialized ones of an
# equal group, and code reaches a variable through the index the loader
# writes in _tls_index and the variable's offset in .tls. the TLS directory
# names the template's start and the end of its initialized bytes, the index
# cell, the callback array and the zero fill, the bytes past that end, and
# its characteristics state the template's alignment. a CRT supplies the
# directory as _tls_used, with _tls_index beside it and the callbacks between
# its .CRT$XLA and .CRT$XLZ bounds, and the image's TLS directory names that
# definition. a link that holds thread-local data and whose inputs define no
# _tls_used supplies them itself: the image reserves the directory in .rdata
# and the index cell in .data, _tls_index is
# a bound symbol in that cell, and the link makes the .CRT$XLA and .CRT$XLZ
# bounds, each a null pointer, so callbacks in .CRT$XL sections between them
# form a closed array, and a callback section that is not read-only data, as
# they are, would lie outside them and is refused. the directory's addresses
# are base relocation sites of an image the loader may place anywhere. a
# machine whose C symbols carry a prefix spells these names otherwise, which
# the link does not hold yet, so its thread-local data is refused. a link that
# needs what the image does not hold yet, a delay loaded import or
# thread-local data in a DLL, is refused naming what it needs.
#
# a DLL is an image of a kind a loader maps into another image: its file
# header states IMAGE_FILE_DLL, it starts at no entry, so its entry point is
# zero (PE Format, Optional Header Standard Fields), and it states no
# terminal server awareness, which describes an application. a DLL binds
# every reference at link time or through an import, so one that refers to
# what nothing defines is refused. its exports, as its inputs' export
# directives state them, are the export table, which the image reserves as
# .edata at the end of the read-only data and the export directory names,
# and the import library a later link binds against it through is its
# interface file, a short import object per export no PRIVATE leaves out.

